# Badges I–M: checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.inventing
@note The 2026 update revised the interview requirement (finding and interviewing a local inventor with a buddy). Excerpts showed the topics but not their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Inventing covers how inventions happen: what inventors do and their role in the economy, interviewing an inventor, patents and intellectual property, improving an existing product, designing, prototyping and testing your own invention, and joining clubs or visiting exhibits.

@owner mb.journalism
@complete complete
@note The 2026 update added an online-article option and a new careers requirement.
@desc Journalism explores how news is gathered and shared: press freedom and ethics, comparing news coverage across media, writing hard news and features, covering a public event, and journalism careers.
1 k Press freedom and ethics :: Freedom of the press, the First Amendment, fact versus opinion, and libel and slander.
2 a Exploring the media :: One option: print and online journalism, or radio and television, including comparing coverage and visiting a newsroom or station.
3 a Writing :: Hard news versus features, the five Ws and H, and a writing option such as an article or interview.
4 a Covering an event :: Attending a public event and producing articles, a broadcast-style package or a photo story.
5 a Careers :: Journalism careers and researching one of them.

@owner mb.kayaking
@complete complete
@note Requirement 2 is the swimmer test, which must be completed before requirements 3 through 8. Confirm sub-item lettering on the official page.
@desc Kayaking teaches safe paddling: hazards and first aid, Safety Afloat, life jackets and safety gear, kayak and paddle design and care, wet exits and rescues, and the strokes and maneuvers of a solo paddler.
@safety Paddling follows Safety Afloat under a qualified supervisor, and you must pass the swimmer test first.
1 k! Hazards, first aid and Safety Afloat :: Likely paddling hazards, first aid for common injuries, and the Safety Afloat policy.
= mb.swimming.1
2 demo! The swimmer test :: Passing the Scouting America swimmer test before the on-water requirements.
3 demo Life jackets and safety gear :: Choosing and fitting a life jacket, and gear such as a signal device, spare paddle and throw bag.
4 k Kayak design and care :: Parts of a kayak, the main kinds of kayaks, and care and storage.
5 k Paddles :: Paddle parts, sizing, use and care.
6 demo! Capsize and rescue :: Wet exits, assisted reentry, a kayak-over-kayak rescue, the HELP position, and swimming a kayak to shore.
7 demo Strokes :: The basic solo strokes, such as forward, back, sweep and draw.
8 demo Maneuvers :: Paddling straight, turning, moving sideways, stopping, reversing and a figure-eight course.

@owner mb.landscape-architecture
@complete complete
@desc Landscape Architecture is the design of outdoor places: visiting a designed landscape, analyzing how it works for people, choosing plants for your region, measuring and redesigning a real entry area, and careers in the field.
1 a Visiting a landscape :: Visiting a completed landscape designed by a landscape architect.
2 d Analyzing the design :: How the site’s spaces, structures, plants and seating serve the people who use it.
3 a Choosing plants :: Shrubs, trees and a ground cover that suit your area and how you would use them.
4 a A redesign :: Measuring and drawing a building entry to scale and redesigning it to be safer and more comfortable.
5 a Careers :: Landscape architecture careers and researching one of them.

@owner mb.law
@complete complete
@desc Law explores where laws come from and how the legal system works: legal history, civil and criminal law, public opinion, consumer protection, courts and mock trials, lawyers and judges, legal aid, and the many areas of law and careers in it.
1 k What law is :: What law is, where it comes from and the purposes it serves.
2 k Legal history :: Options such as ancient law codes and the Magna Carta, the jury system or famous trials.
3 k Civil and criminal law :: The differences between civil and criminal law, with examples.
4 a Public opinion :: Asking several people about the role of law enforcement and reporting back.
5 k Consumer protection :: Laws and organizations that protect consumers and sellers.
6 a A court experience :: One option: attending a court session and summarizing it, or organizing a mock trial.
7 a Visiting a lawyer :: Meeting a lawyer who works for a business or government agency.
8 k Lawyers and judges :: How to become a lawyer in your state and how judges are chosen.
9 a Law careers :: Law-related jobs and which you would prefer and why.
10 k Getting legal help :: Where people can find a lawyer, including free legal help.
11 k Areas of law :: The importance of several areas of law, such as administrative law or dispute resolution.

@owner mb.leatherwork
@complete complete
@desc Leatherwork is a hands-on craft badge: tool safety and first aid, where leather comes from and its types, leather projects using a range of techniques, braiding, and exploring tanning, leather care or a leather business.
1 k! Safety and first aid :: Hazards of leatherwork tools and materials, and first aid for cuts, punctures and chemical reactions.
2 k Leather and hides :: Where leather comes from, the hides used and the main types of leather.
3 a Leather projects :: Making leather items that use several techniques, such as cutting, stamping, dyeing and lacing.
4 a Braiding :: Braiding or plaiting with leather, vinyl lace or paracord.
5 a Exploring leather :: One option, such as researching tanning, tanning a hide, restoring leather goods or visiting a leather business.

@owner mb.mammal-study
@complete complete
@note The 2026 update added a careers option as requirement 7 and changed the size of the study area in one option of requirement 3; the official web page and the change notice disagreed on the size, so check the current text.
@desc Mammal Study explores the animals most like us: what makes a mammal, how animals are classified, studying mammals in the field, tracks, photography and museums, a project that helps mammal populations, outdoor ethics, and careers.
1 k What a mammal is :: Animals, invertebrates, vertebrates and mammals, and what sets mammals apart.
2 k Classification :: How the animal kingdom is classified, from phylum to species.
3 a Field study :: One option: studying mammals in two habitats, in one small area over several days, or a life history.
4 a Learning about mammals :: One option, such as photographing mammals, a tracking pit, a museum visit or a book report.
5 s A mammal project :: A project that affects the numbers of one or more kinds of mammals.
6 d Outdoor ethics :: Leave No Trace and the Outdoor Code as they apply to mammal study.
7 a Careers or hobbies :: One option, including a career related to mammals.

@owner mb.metalwork
@note Excerpts showed the structure (safety, metal science, work-hardening and annealing, career or hobby research, and one project track: sheet metal, silversmithing, founding or blacksmithing) but not full numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Metalwork covers the science and craft of shaping metal: shop safety, alloys and heat treatment, basic metalworking exercises, and one craft track: sheet metal, silversmithing, casting or blacksmithing.
@safety Metalwork involves heat, sharp edges and molten metal: wear eye, hand and foot protection and work only under a qualified adult.
